{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a non-inductive methodological rule grounded in verisimilitude and the logic of severe testing, not probability.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude cannot be formally defined without probabilistic measures (as per Tichý/Miller), this defense collapses.","If the preference implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature to justify acting on past success, it smuggles in induction."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper distinguishes between logical probability (which remains zero or undefined for universal laws) and corroboration (a report of past performance). The preference is rational because severe tests eliminate false theories, leaving those with higher potential truth-content (verisimilitude). Acting on the best-tested theory is the only rational strategy to approximate truth, even without a guarantee of success or an inductive inference that the future will resemble the past.","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","5bcef9fd7b590859587c7a90d1eb7e0d84c626f0dce1a881500922f80e403db7","76cf918960284a1cad58c407e26c2ac89253cefdaf9fa848c56f60e4fab131c8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles in induction, rendering Popper's position internally inconsistent when moving from logic to action.","counterconditions":["If Popper can ground the preference in a non-inductive principle like 'minimize known error' without assuming future success.","If the concept of 'rational action' can be decoupled from expectations of future performance."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"To prefer theory A over B because A has survived tests while B has not (or fewer) implies an expectation that A will continue to succeed where B might fail. This expectation relies on the assumption that the future will resemble the past (uniformity of nature), which is the principle of induction Hume identified and Popper sought to reject. Without this inductive leap, the fact that A survived yesterday offers no rational basis for betting on A today.","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","5bcef9fd7b590859587c7a90d1eb7e0d84c626f0dce1a881500922f80e403db7","76cf918960284a1cad58c407e26c2ac89253cefdaf9fa848c56f60e4fab131c8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible not as induction nor as verisimilitude, but as a pragmatic evolutionary imperative: science selects for survival of theories, analogous to natural selection, where 'rationality' is redefined as adaptive fitness rather than logical justification.","counterconditions":["If this reduces science to mere survival without truth-seeking, abandoning Popper's realism.","If 'adaptive fitness' is indistinguishable from inductive success in practice."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Popper's epistemology is evolutionary. Theories compete; severe tests are the environment. Preferencing the survivor is not an inductive inference about the future, nor a claim about absolute truth, but a recognition of the only available heuristic for survival in an uncertain world. It is a 'biological/psychological inevitability' elevated to a methodological principle because it is the only strategy that doesn't lead to immediate extinction (error).","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","5bcef9fd7b590859587c7a90d1eb7e0d84c626f0dce1a881500922f80e403db7","76cf918960284a1cad58c407e26c2ac89253cefdaf9fa848c56f60e4fab131c8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is a false dichotomy; the preference is a 'critical rationalist wager' that transcends the induction/probability binary by treating corroboration as a report of informational content rather than predictive power.","counterconditions":["If acting on high-content theories still requires assuming they will hold in new contexts (induction).","If 'informational content' cannot be separated from 'probability of being true' in decision making."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Corroboration reports that a theory has high empirical content (it risked much and survived). Preferring it is preferring boldness and precision. This is not induction (expecting repetition) but a commitment to learning: the theory that says more and survives is more informative. Acting on it is a bet on information density, not statistical frequency.","optional_refs":["a9f9ff400459ab6e301346b3d488c3b28dbc247dda9fc511e0bb8b1504a0ba48","66225d91beb60a53b7f8f9c10281638da03df4b726808e77b4bfb8db6cd0919e","4c1f39b88c2aff008393b049ab69e2d3916b241903dc9cf1cba92d89c4bd3a0a","5bcef9fd7b590859587c7a90d1eb7e0d84c626f0dce1a881500922f80e403db7","76cf918960284a1cad58c407e26c2ac89253cefdaf9fa848c56f60e4fab131c8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}